+++
title: Akkadian Empire
description: State in Mesopotamia (c. 2334–2154 BC)
hook: Akkad, the empire's capital, has not yet been located. There has been
  much speculation about where it stood, and written sources from the Sargonic
  period are uncommon partly because the city is still missing.
standfirst: An ancient kingdom established around 2334 BCE and called the
  first empire in world history, centred on the city of Akkad in modern-day
  Iraq. It united Akkadian and Sumerian speakers under one rule and sent
  military expeditions as far south as Dilmun and Magan.
key-facts:
  - name: Capital
    value: Akkad — a city that has not yet been located
  - name: Founded
    value: c. 2334 BC, in the Bronze Age
  - name: Ended
    value: c. 2154 BC
  - name: First and last kings
    value: Sargon (c. 2334–2279 BC) and Shu-turul (c. 2168–2154 BC)
  - name: Languages
    value: Akkadian (dominant); Sumerian (vernacular, and honorific and liturgical)
  - name: Land it covered, today
    value: Iraq, Iran, Syria, Turkey and Kuwait
pull-quotes:
  - text: Naram-Sin, the mighty, king of Agade, when the four quarters together
      revolted against him, through the love which the goddess Astar showed
      him, he was victorious in nine battles in one in 1 year, and the kings
      whom they (the rebels[?]) had raised (against him), he captured.
    attribution: Inscription on the Bassetki Statue, the copper base of a Narim-Sin statue
    section: contemporary-epigraphic-sources
  - text: My changeling mother conceived me, in secret she bore me. She set me in
      a basket of rushes, with bitumen she sealed my lid. She cast me into the
      river which rose not over me. The river bore me up and carried me to
      Akki, the drawer of water.
    attribution: One legend of Sargon from Neo-Assyrian times
    section: sargon-of-akkad
  - text: Enlil brought out of the mountains those who do not resemble other
      people, who are not reckoned as part of the Land, the Gutians, an
      unbridled people, with human intelligence but canine instincts and
      monkeys' features.
    attribution: Cursing of Agade, composed in the Ur III period
    section: later-copies-and-literary-compositions
  - text: Who was king? Who was not king? Irgigi the king; Nanum, the king; Imi
      the king; Ilulu, the king—the four of them were kings but reigned only
      three years.
    attribution: The Sumerian King List
    section: collapse
  - text: '"Sargon, the king, to whom Enlil permitted no rival—5,400 warriors ate
      bread daily before him".'
    attribution: A tablet quoted in the article's account of the Akkadian economy
    section: economy
timeline:
  - when: Circa 2350 BC
    what: Akkad is still unexpanded; Sumer is held by its last king, Lugal-Zage-Si.
    from: Akkad before expansion (in green). The territory of Sumer under its last
      king Lugal-Zage-Si appears in orange. Circa 2350 BC
  - when: c. 2340 – c. 2284 BC
    what: The conquests of Sargon of Akkad.
    from: "conquests-of-sargon-of-akkad: c. 2340 – c. 2284 BC"
  - when: c. 2334 BC
    what: The empire is established.
    from: "established: c. 2334 BC"
  - when: c. 2334–2279 BC
    what: Sargon, the first king.
    from: "c-2334-2279-bc: Sargon (first)"
  - when: 2278–2270 BC
    what: Rimush reigns nine years, fights hard to retain the empire, and is
      assassinated by some of his own courtiers.
    from: revolts broke out during the nine-year reign of Rimush (2278–2270 BC),
      who fought hard to retain the empire, and was successful until he was
      assassinated by some of his own courtiers.
  - when: 2269–2255 BC
    what: Manishtushu, Rimush's elder brother, succeeds him.
    from: Rimush's elder brother, Manishtushu (2269–2255 BC) succeeded him.
  - when: 2254–2218 BC
    what: Naram-Sin assumes the imperial title "King Naram-Sin, king of the
      four-quarters".
    from: Manishtushu's son and successor, Naram-Sin (2254–2218 BC), due to vast
      military conquests, assumed the imperial title "King Naram-Sin, king of
      the four-quarters" (Lugal Naram-Sîn, Šar kibrat 'arbaim), the
      four-quarters as a reference to the entire world.
  - when: 2260 BC
    what: Victory over the Lullubi from Zagros, celebrated on the Victory Stele of
      Naram-Sin.
    from: Victory Stele of Naram-Sin, celebrating victory against the Lullubi from
      Zagros 2260 BC.
  - when: At 2200 BC
    what: A marked increase in aridity and wind circulation, after a volcanic
      eruption, degrades land-use conditions — Harvey Weiss's reading of the
      soil data.
    from: At 2200 BC, a marked increase in aridity and wind circulation,
      subsequent to a volcanic eruption, induced a considerable degradation of
      land-use conditions.
  - when: c. 2168–2154 BC
    what: Shu-turul, the last king.
    from: "c-2168-2154-bc: Shu-turul (last)"
  - when: c. 2154 BC
    what: The empire is dissolved.
    from: "disestablished: c. 2154 BC"
  - when: 2112–2095 BC
    what: The Sumerian king Ur-Nammu clears the Gutians from Mesopotamia.
    from: The Sumerian king Ur-Nammu (2112–2095 BC) cleared the Gutians from
      Mesopotamia during his reign.
image-plan:
  - file: Bronze_head_of_an_Akkadian_ruler,_discovered_in_Nineveh_in_1931,_presumably_depicting_either_Sargon_or_Sargon's_grandson_Naram-Sin_(Rijksmuseum_van_Oudheden).jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "A bronze head of an Akkadian ruler, dug up at Nineveh in 1931.
      Nobody can say for certain who it is: presumably either Sargon or, more
      probably, his grandson Naram-Sin."
  - file: Umma2350.PNG
    role: inline
    section: pre-sargonic-akkad
    caption: Akkad before expansion, in green, with the territory of Sumer under
      its last king Lugal-Zage-Si in orange. Circa 2350 BC.
  - file: Sargon_on_his_victory_stele.jpg
    role: full
    section: sargon-of-akkad
    caption: 'Sargon on his victory stele: a royal hair bun, a mace, a flounced
      royal coat over the left shoulder and a large belt, with an attendant
      behind him holding a royal umbrella. His name in cuneiform, "King
      Sargon", is still faintly readable in front of his face. Louvre Museum.'
  - file: Akkadian_official_in_the_retinue_of_Sargon_of_Akkad,_holding_an_axe.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sargon-of-akkad
    caption: An Akkadian official in Sargon's retinue, carrying an axe.
  - file: Prisoners_on_the_victory_stele_of_an_Akkadian_king_circa_2300_BCE_Louvre_Museum_Sb_3.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sargon-of-akkad
    caption: Prisoners escorted by a soldier on a victory stele of Sargon, circa
      2300 BC. Their hairstyle — curly on top, short at the sides — is
      characteristic of Sumerians, as also seen on the Standard of Ur. Louvre
      Museum.
  - file: P1150890_Louvre_stèle_de_victoire_Akkad_AO2678_rwk.jpg
    role: inline
    section: rimush-and-manishtushu
    caption: Akkadian soldiers slaying enemies, circa 2300 BC, possibly from a
      Victory Stele of Rimush — the king who kept meticulous records of his
      destructions.
  - file: Stele_of_Naram-Sin_of_Akkad.jpg
    role: inline
    section: naram-sin
    caption: A portrait of Naram-Sin, with an inscription in his name.
  - file: Victory_stele_of_Naram_Sin_9068.jpg
    role: full
    section: naram-sin
    caption: The Victory Stele of Naram-Sin, celebrating victory against the
      Lullubi from Zagros, 2260 BC. He wears a horned helmet, a symbol of
      divinity, and is portrayed on a larger scale than the others to
      emphasize his superiority. It was carried off from Sippar to Susa as war
      prize in the 12th century BC.
  - file: TellBrakNaramsinN.jpg
    role: inline
    section: naram-sin
    caption: The palace of Naram-Sin at Tell Brak, the royal residence he built to
      better police Syria.
  - file: The_Gutians_capturing_a_Babylonian_city.jpg
    role: full
    section: collapse
    caption: The Gutians capturing a Babylonian city as the Akkadians make a stand
      outside it — a 19th century illustration, not an ancient one.
  - file: Akkadian_-_Cylinder_Seal_with_King_or_God_and_Vanquished_Lion_-_Walters_42674_-_Side_D.jpg
    role: inline
    section: seals
    caption: '"Cylinder Seal with King or God and Vanquished Lion" (Old Akkadian).
      The Walters Art Museum.'
  - file: Impression_of_an_Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_The_Divine_Sharkalisharri_Prince_of_Akkad_Ibni-Sharrum_the_Scribe_his_servant.jpg
    role: inline
    section: foreign-trade
    caption: A seal of the time of Sharkalisharri (c. 2200 BC), inscribed "The
      Divine Sharkalisharri Prince of Akkad, Ibni-Sharrum the Scribe his
      servant". The long-horned buffalo is thought to have come from the Indus
      Valley, and testifies to exchanges with Meluhha. Circa 2217–2193 BC.
      Louvre Museum.
  - file: Akkadian_Empire_soldiers_on_the_victory_stele_of_Naram-Sin_circa_2250_BC.jpg
    role: inline
    section: government
    caption: Akkadian Empire soldiers on the Victory Stele of Naram-Sin, circa
      2250 BC.
  - file: Cylinder_seal_of_the_scribe_Kalki_(photograph).jpg
    role: inline
    section: economy
    caption: "The cylinder seal of the scribe Kalki: Prince Ubil-Eshtar, probable
      brother of Sargon, with an archer in front, the scribe holding a tablet
      behind him, and two dignitaries with weapons."
  - file: Murex_Rimush_Louvre_AO21404.jpg
    role: inline
    section: economy
    caption: A murex shell bearing the name of Rimush, king of Kish, c. 2270 BC.
      It was traded from the Mediterranean coast, where Canaanites used the
      shells to make a purple dye. Louvre.
  - file: Moyen_Orient_3mil_aC.svg
    role: full
    section: foreign-trade
    caption: "The foreign lands as the Mesopotamians located them: Elam, Magan,
      Dilmun, Marhashi and Meluhha."
  - file: Enheduanna,_daughter_of_Sargon_of_Akkad.jpg
    role: full
    section: poetpriestess-enheduanna
    caption: Enheduanna, daughter of Sargon of Akkad, circa 2300 BC — the first
      poet in history whose name is known.
  - file: Ishtar_on_an_Akkadian_seal.jpg
    role: inline
    section: poetpriestess-enheduanna
    caption: The goddess Ishtar on an Akkadian seal, 2350–2150 BC. Enheduanna's
      hymns to Inanna unite the warlike Akkadian Ishtar's qualities to those
      of the gentler Sumerian goddess.
glossary:
  - term: ensi
    gloss: Traditionally the highest functionary of a Sumerian city-state; under
      Sargon the ensis generally kept their positions but were seen more as
      provincial governors.
    from: Traditionally, the ensi was the highest functionary of the Sumerian
      city-states. ... Under Sargon, the ensis generally retained their
      positions, but were seen more as provincial governors.
  - term: lugal
    gloss: "Literally lu = man, gal = Great: the monarch, at first subordinate to
      the priestly ensi and appointed at times of troubles, but by later
      dynastic times the preeminent role, with his own palace independent of
      the temple."
    from: Initially, the monarchical lugal (lu = man, gal =Great) was subordinate
      to the priestly ensi, and was appointed at times of troubles, but by
      later dynastic times, it was the lugal who had emerged as the preeminent
      role, having his own "é" (= house) or "palace", independent from the
      temple establishment.
  - term: šar kiššati
    gloss: '"King of Kish": whichever dynasty controlled Kish was recognised by
      this title and considered preeminent in Sumer; under Sargon it became
      recognised as meaning "lord of the universe".'
    from: By the time of Mesalim, whichever dynasty controlled the city of Kish
      was recognised as šar kiššati (= king of Kish), and was considered
      preeminent in Sumer, possibly because this was where the two rivers
      approached, and whoever controlled Kish ultimately controlled the
      irrigation systems of the other cities downstream.
  - term: year-name
    gloss: A calendar year named after something that happened in it, used from
      Sargonic times until the end of the Old Babylonian period — for example,
      "Year in which the divine Hammu[rabi] the king Esznunna destroyed by a
      flood."
    from: Before the Akkadian Empire, calendar years were marked by Regnal
      Numbers. During Sargonic times, a system of year-names was used. This
      practice continued until the end of the Old Babylonian period, for
      example, "Year in which the divine Hammu[rabi] the king Esznunna
      destroyed by a flood."
  - term: middle chronology
    gloss: The dating scheme that puts the Akkadian period at 2334–2154 BC; the
      rival short-chronology dates of 2270–2083 BC are now considered less
      likely.
    from: The Akkadian period is generally dated to 2334–2154 BC (according to the
      middle chronology). The short-chronology dates of 2270–2083 BC are now
      considered less likely.
  - term: 4.2 kiloyear event
    gloss: A global centennial-scale drought sometimes given this name, which one
      theory — still controversial — associates with the decline at the end of
      the Akkadian period.
    from: One theory, which remains controversial, associates regional decline at
      the end of the Akkadian period (and of the First Intermediary Period
      following the Old Kingdom in Ancient Egypt) with rapidly increasing
      aridity, and failing rainfall in the region of the Ancient Near East,
      caused by a global centennial-scale drought, sometimes called the 4.2
      kiloyear event.
  - term: sprachbund
    gloss: What scholars have taken to calling Sumerian and Akkadian in the third
      millennium, given how far each influenced the other — lexical borrowing
      on a massive scale, and syntactic, morphological and phonological
      convergence.
    from: The influence of Sumerian on Akkadian (and vice versa) is evident in all
      areas, from lexical borrowing on a massive scale, to syntactic,
      morphological, and phonological convergence. This has prompted scholars
      to refer to Sumerian and Akkadian in the third millennium as a
      sprachbund.
  - term: the Upper Country
    gloss: The rain-fed farmland of northern Iraq, one of the region's two
      principal agricultural centres.
    from: 'The population of Akkad, like nearly all pre-modern states, was
      entirely dependent upon the agricultural systems of the region, which
      seem to have had two principal centres: the irrigated farmlands of
      southern Iraq that traditionally had a yield of 30 grains returned for
      each grain sown and the rain-fed agriculture of northern Iraq, known as
      the "Upper Country."'
further:
  - slug: gutian-rule-in-mesopotamia
    why: The Great Rebellion Against Naram-Sin and the Cursing of Agade — the two
      compositions this article quotes — are told there at length.
  - slug: gutians
    why: They took control of central Babylonia in the power vacuum after Akkad,
      and little is known about their period or how long it endured.
  - slug: lagash
    why: As Akkadian power waned, Puzer-Mama achieved independence from
      Shar-Kali-Sharri, took the title "King of Lagash" and started the Second
      Dynasty of Lagash.
anchors:
  hook: Epigraphic sources from the Sargonic (Akkadian Empire) period are
    uncommon, partly because the capital Akkad, like the capitals of the later
    Mitanni and Sealand, has not yet been located, though there has been much
    speculation.
  standfirst: The Akkadian Empire or the Kingdom of Akkad/Agade was an ancient
    kingdom established around 2334 BCE, and the first empire in world
    history, succeeding the long-lived city-states of Sumer. Centered on the
    city of Akkad and its surrounding region in modern-day Iraq, the empire
    united the Semitic Akkadian and Sumerian speakers under one rule and
    exercised significant influence across Mesopotamia, the Levant, modern-day
    Iran and Anatolia, sending military expeditions as far south as Dilmun and
    Magan in the Arabian Peninsula.
